 #uuid "35c6e3db-073b-5867-8137-1d8660b20887" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192923 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186193660 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192923 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186193661 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192923 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186193671 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192923 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186193672 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192923 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186193680 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192923 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186193698 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192923 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192923 #edb/ref 1045 "Computing Partial Derivatives Using the Rules of Differentiation" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192923 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186193672 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192923 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048630 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192923 #edb/ref 1050 960.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186192923 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "c9c41155-2ae1-5832-8b1b-927ecf980d8f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193191 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186277976 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193191 #edb/ref 1069 "q-159751" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193191 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193191 #edb/ref 1071 "For the function $f(x,y)=y{e}^{xy}-{x}^{2}{y}^{2},$ evaluate $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}$ at the point $(1,1).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193191 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d20c624a-1709-5495-88ea-45d0df97ea34" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193191 #edb/ref 1074 "We differentiate the given function with respect to $y,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nFirst, we use the sum and constant multiple rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y{e}^{xy}-{x}^{2}{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y{e}^{xy})-\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y{e}^{xy})-{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the product, the chain, and the constant multiple rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y{e}^{xy})-{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}) & =(\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y)\\cdot {e}^{xy}+y\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{xy}))-{x}^{2}\\cdot (2y) \\\\  & =(1\\cdot {e}^{xy}+y\\cdot {e}^{xy}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy))-2{x}^{2}y \\\\  & =({e}^{xy}+y\\cdot {e}^{xy}\\cdot x\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(y))-2{x}^{2}y \\\\  & =({e}^{xy}+y\\cdot {e}^{xy}\\cdot x\\cdot 1)-2{x}^{2}y \\\\  & ={e}^{xy}+xy{e}^{xy}-2{x}^{2}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point $(1,1):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂y}{|}_{(1,1)} & ={e}^{1\\cdot 1}+1\\cdot 1\\cdot {e}^{1\\cdot 1}-2\\cdot {1}^{2}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =e+e-2 \\\\  & =2e-2\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193320 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186323292 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193320 #edb/ref 1069 "q-35710" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193320 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193320 #edb/ref 1071 "For the function $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}{y}^{2}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xz),$ evaluate $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}$ at the point $(1,0,1).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193320 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3cd9c82e-fba1-518a-8285-94d9c7befa44" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193320 #edb/ref 1074 "We differentiate the given function with respect to $y,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nFirst, we use the sum and constant multiple rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}{y}^{2}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xz)) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}{y}^{2})-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xz)) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2})-(0) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\cdot (2y) \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the partial derivative at the point $(1,0,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂y}{|}_{(1,0,1)} & =2(1)^{2}(0) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193660 #edb/ref 1061 41900 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193660 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8f9e1a5f-2ddb-5dcf-bdb9-12c97e29ab65" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193660 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186192784 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193661 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186193680 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193661 #edb/ref 1061 41903 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193661 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d18aca64-f3ac-5a3a-837c-b8cb8fa44772" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193661 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186192785 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193671 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186193661 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193671 #edb/ref 1061 41983 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193671 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2a951cc5-70fa-5cd6-a379-4cfe861c71d4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193671 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186192790 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193672 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186193671 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193672 #edb/ref 1061 41989 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193672 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c3b7bd9a-f6d4-521a-ab11-4ff2cecfbe07" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193672 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186193890 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193680 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186193698 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193680 #edb/ref 1061 42238 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193680 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4bae0aea-9643-5e83-9379-45ac0682c9ca" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193680 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186192794 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193698 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186193660 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193698 #edb/ref 1061 44640 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193698 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1014e39c-1f7c-5989-8916-134b9e90b165" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193698 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186193907 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193890 #edb/ref 1064 "We can use the chain, product, and quotient rules to find partial derivatives of multivariable functions. \n\nFor example, consider the following function: \n\n$$\nf(x,y) = \\cos(xy^2) + 3x\n$$\n\nTo compute the partial derivative of $f$ with respect to $x,$ we \"fix\" the variable ${\\color{blue}y}$ (in other words, we treat ${\\color{blue}y}$ as if it were constant). Then, we take the derivative with respect to $x,$ as usual.\n\nFirst, we use the sum rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x{y}^{2})+3x) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x{y}^{2}))+\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the chain rule and the constant multiple rules for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x{y}^{2}))+\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3x) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x{y}^{2})\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3x) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x{y}^{2})\\cdot {y}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x)+3\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x{y}^{2})\\cdot {y}^{2}\\cdot 1+3\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =3-{y}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x{y}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo compute the partial derivative of $f$ with respect to $y,$ we \"fix\" the variable ${\\color{blue}x}$ (in other words, we treat ${\\color{blue}x}$ as if it were constant). Then, we take the derivative with respect to $y,$ as usual.\n\nFirst, we use the sum rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x{y}^{2})+3x) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x{y}^{2}))+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the chain rule and the constant multiple rules for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x{y}^{2}))+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3x) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x{y}^{2})\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{y}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3x) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x{y}^{2})\\cdot x\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2})+0 \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x{y}^{2})\\cdot x\\cdot 2y \\\\  & =-2xy\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x{y}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193890 #edb/ref 1065 6937 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193890 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "66f2199e-b3f8-5590-80f3-bbac79027096" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193890 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193907 #edb/ref 1064 "We can also compute partial derivatives of functions with three or even more variables. \n\nFor example, suppose we have the function $f(x,y,z).$ We find its partial derivatives as follows:\n\n- To compute $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x},$ we differentiate with respect to $x,$ treating $y$ *and* $z$ as constants.\n\n- To compute $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y},$ we differentiate with respect to $y,$ treating $x$ *and* $z$ as constants.\n\n- To compute $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial z},$ we differentiate with respect to $z,$ treating $x$ *and* $y$ as constants.\n\nLet's see an example." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193907 #edb/ref 1065 7692 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193907 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "c87e2fef-e77f-5c67-a502-0d900d85ee40" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193907 #edb/ref 1067 "Partial Derivatives of Three-Variable Functions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186275530 #edb/ref 1080 "{x}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+4{x}^{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-x{y}^{3}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186275530 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186275530 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "05f76324-5a68-5230-a5cd-bf08feffd775" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186275645 #edb/ref 1080 "1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186275645 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186275645 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "124ba60a-ec47-5dd9-97fc-d33ccf7d0e0b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186275864 #edb/ref 1080 "-3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186275864 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186275864 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2a78e326-4c51-50ac-aad3-5c443d334667" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186276034 #edb/ref 1080 "-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{2xy-1})}{2x\\sqrt{2xy-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186276034 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 